1,222,788 -> 330,456 (3.70x) at the SAME footprint. The whole win is ticks:
~100 local ticks/char -> ~10 glyphs/char.

DESIGN: the entire 32-deep stack is ONE REGISTER. v = 3v + d with d in {1,2,3},
so v = 0 means empty and no sentinel is needed; pop is w = v - d, and valid iff
w >= 0 and w % 3 == 0, which catches underflow AND shallow mismatches together.
Widest reachable word (3^33-3)/2 = 2.78e15, three orders inside 2^63. No ring,
no tape, no rotation.

The stack lives in B, which `r` never clobbers, so the worker never spills — and
that is exactly why there are THREE MEN: the worker can never classify, because
`M` would overwrite the stack, and the position is a fourth quantity. CLASS is
stateless (B=5 loaded once in INIT, never touched in the loop, so per char it is
`r b } x`); WORK holds the stack; COUNT holds position in B and remaining in BP,
counting the worker's acks so at a failure it holds exactly i-1.

Classifier: "openers are even, closers odd" is FALSE ([ = 91, { = 123). The real
identities are `c >> 5` giving type 1/2/3 for all six codes, and closer iff
(bit0, bit1) = (1, 0).

THE BUG THAT COST A SUBMISSION (20/26, one PUBLIC case wrong):
An arrowhead with a room wall behind it IS THE MOUTH OF A PIPE leaving that room,
intended or not. Two cells of the routed `tok` corridor turned south directly
under CLASS's south wall, so the runtime built SEVEN pipes where `analyze`
reported five; CLASS's four `s` glyphs bound three different queues by nearest
column and WORK read tokens out of order. Minimal repro `{}({)` -> 6, want 5;
nothing below length 5 fails. route_pipe now refuses any cell that would become a
mouth, and check_no_phantom_pipes counts mouths the way the runtime does. Walls
must come from the floor plan, not the glyphs, because `|` and `-` are a pipe's
body as well as a room's wall — that ambiguity is where the phantom hides.

WHY IT WAS NOT CAUGHT: scoring.score_program compared output LENGTH, not content
(fixed in 22a081d), and FastLittleman disagrees with the reference engine on this
grid. tests/test_pipe_mouths.py isolates that in nine lines: the reference engine
refuses to load a grid ("the output room has more than one incoming pipe") that
FastLittleman loads and reports as 1 pipe. The phantom only materialises when its
mouth precedes the real mouth in row-major order.

The slow tier for this problem is now the reference engine and nothing else.
